WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:04.820
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.820 --> 00:00:09.990
Most people think that if we have a piece
of camera footage that is a little choppy,

00:00:09.990 --> 00:00:13.929
then there is nothing we can do with it, we
better throw it away.

00:00:13.929 --> 00:00:15.049
Is that true?

00:00:15.049 --> 00:00:17.080
No, not at all!

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:21.620
Earlier, we discussed two potential techniques
to remedy this common problem.

00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:28.070
The problem statement is simple, in goes a
choppy video, something happens, and then,

00:00:28.070 --> 00:00:30.560
out comes a smooth and creamy video.

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:36.769
This process is often referred to as frame
interpolation, or frame inbetweening.

00:00:36.769 --> 00:00:39.579
And of course, it’s easier said than done.

00:00:39.579 --> 00:00:45.170
If it works well, it really looks like magic
- much like in the science fiction movies.

00:00:45.170 --> 00:00:49.979
So what are the potential somethings that
we can use to make this happen?

00:00:49.979 --> 00:00:52.120
One, optical flow.

00:00:52.120 --> 00:00:57.269
This is an originally handcrafted method that
tries to predict the motion that takes place

00:00:57.269 --> 00:00:58.829
between these frames.

00:00:58.829 --> 00:01:04.989
This can kind of produce new information and
I use this in these videos on a regular basis,

00:01:04.989 --> 00:01:10.800
but the output footage also has to be carefully
inspected for unwanted artifacts.

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:13.430
Which are a relatively common occurrence.

00:01:13.430 --> 00:01:18.910
Two, we can also try to give a bunch of training
data to a neural network, and teach it to

00:01:18.910 --> 00:01:21.390
perform this frame inbetweening.

00:01:21.390 --> 00:01:25.090
And if we do, the results are magnificent.

00:01:25.090 --> 00:01:27.000
We can do so much with this!

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:32.840
But wait a second…if we can do this for
video frames… here is a crazy idea - how

00:01:32.840 --> 00:01:38.180
about a similar kind of inbetweening…for
animating humanoids?

00:01:38.180 --> 00:01:43.090
That would really be something else and it
would save us so much time and work!

00:01:43.090 --> 00:01:46.870
Let’s see what this new method can do in
this area!

00:01:46.870 --> 00:01:52.090
The value proposition of this technique is
as simple as it gets: we set up a bunch of

00:01:52.090 --> 00:01:58.060
keyframes, these are the transparent figures,
and the neural network creates realistic motion

00:01:58.060 --> 00:02:01.450
that transitions from one stage to the next
one.

00:02:01.450 --> 00:02:07.159
Look, it really seems to be able to do it
all, it can perform twists and turns, brisk

00:02:07.159 --> 00:02:12.340
walks and runs, and you will see in a minute,
even dance moves.

00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:19.130
Hmm…this inbetweening for animating humanoid
motion idea may not be so crazy after all!

00:02:19.130 --> 00:02:24.060
What’s more, this could be super useful
for artists working in the industry, who can

00:02:24.060 --> 00:02:30.060
not only do all this, but they can also set
up movement variations by moving the keyframes

00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:33.700
around spatially.

00:02:33.700 --> 00:02:39.850
Or we can even set up temporal variations
to create different timings for the movement.

00:02:39.850 --> 00:02:40.870
Excellent.

00:02:40.870 --> 00:02:46.010
Of course, it cannot do everything, if we
set up the intermediate stages in a way that

00:02:46.010 --> 00:02:51.180
uncommon motions would be required to fill
in, we might end up with one of these failure

00:02:51.180 --> 00:02:52.270
cases.

00:02:52.270 --> 00:02:57.401
And all these results depend on how much training
data we have with the kinds of motions we

00:02:57.401 --> 00:02:58.401
need to fill in.

00:02:58.401 --> 00:03:01.170
Let’s have a look at a more detailed example!

00:03:01.170 --> 00:03:08.569
This smooth chap has been given lots of training
data with dancing moves, and…look!

00:03:08.569 --> 00:03:15.700
And when we pull out these dance moves from
his training data, he becomes a drunkard.

00:03:15.700 --> 00:03:18.709
So, talking about training data.

00:03:18.709 --> 00:03:22.720
How much motion capture footage was given
to this algorithm?

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:26.490
It used the Ubisoft La Forge Animation Dataset.

00:03:26.490 --> 00:03:34.290
This contains 5 subjects, 77 sequences, about
4.5 hours of footage in total.

00:03:34.290 --> 00:03:36.599
Wow, that is not that much.

00:03:36.599 --> 00:03:41.739
For instance, it only has 8 movement sequences
for dancing.

00:03:41.739 --> 00:03:43.810
That is not that much at all.

00:03:43.810 --> 00:03:47.070
And we’ve already seen that the model can
dance.

00:03:47.070 --> 00:03:53.460
That is some serious data efficiency, especially
given that it can even climb through obstacles.

00:03:53.460 --> 00:03:56.990
So much knowledge has been extracted from
so little data.

00:03:56.990 --> 00:04:01.300
It truly feels like we are living in a science
fiction world.

00:04:01.300 --> 00:04:02.710
What a time to be alive!

00:04:02.710 --> 00:04:08.770
So, when we write a paper like this, how do
we compare the results to previous techniques?

00:04:08.770 --> 00:04:11.780
How can we decide which technique is better?

00:04:11.780 --> 00:04:15.220
Well, the level 1 solution is a user study.

00:04:15.220 --> 00:04:20.380
We call some folks in, show them the footage,
ask which one they liked best - the previous

00:04:20.380 --> 00:04:22.250
method, or this one?

00:04:22.250 --> 00:04:28.270
That would work, but of course, it is quite
laborious, but fortunately, there is a level

00:04:28.270 --> 00:04:29.340
2 solution.

00:04:29.340 --> 00:04:36.030
And this level 2 solution is called the Normalized
Power Spectrum Similarity, NPSS in short.

00:04:36.030 --> 00:04:40.900
This is a number that we can produce with
a computer, no humans are required, and it

00:04:40.900 --> 00:04:44.300
measures how believable these motions are.

00:04:44.300 --> 00:04:49.690
And the key of NPSS is that it correlates
with human judgement, or in other words, if

00:04:49.690 --> 00:04:55.250
this says that a technique is better, then
it is likely that humans would also come to

00:04:55.250 --> 00:04:57.100
the same conclusion.

00:04:57.100 --> 00:04:59.280
So let’s see.

00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:05.940
Here are the previous methods, NPSS is subject
to minimization, in other words, the lower,

00:05:05.940 --> 00:05:06.940
the better.

00:05:06.940 --> 00:05:13.850
And, let’s see the new method…oh yes,
it indeed outpaces the competition.

00:05:13.850 --> 00:05:20.910
So, there is no wonder that this incredible
paper was accepted to the SIGGRAPH ASIA conference.

00:05:20.910 --> 00:05:22.720
What does that mean exactly?

00:05:22.720 --> 00:05:28.380
If research were the olympics, a SIGGRAPH
or SIGGRAPH ASIA paper would be the gold medal.

00:05:28.380 --> 00:05:32.810
And, this was Mr. Felix Harvey’s first few
papers.

00:05:32.810 --> 00:05:34.570
Huge congratulations!

00:05:34.570 --> 00:05:42.310
And as an additional goodie, it can create
an animation of me when I lost my papers,

00:05:42.310 --> 00:05:44.820
and this is me when I found them.

00:05:44.820 --> 00:05:49.680
Do you have some more ideas on how we could
put such an amazing technique to use?

00:05:49.680 --> 00:06:41.810
Let me know in the comments below.

00:06:41.810 --> 00:06:50.060
Thanks 
for watching and for your generous support,

00:06:50.060 --> 00:06:51.629
and I'll see you next time!

